/F2 13 Tf (Flood Response Procedure FRP-3) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
T*
(Issued by the Varnholt Water Board operations directorate. This procedure applies) Tj T*
(whenever the Elske River threatens assets of the board, in particular the stations that) Tj T*
(sit inside the mapped flood plain.) Tj T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf (1. Purpose and scope) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
T*
(The procedure sets out who decides, who acts and in what order when river levels rise.) Tj T*
(It covers protection of pumping stations, continuity of supply, communication with the) Tj T*
(public and the return to normal service. It does not cover sewer flooding, which is) Tj T*
(handled by the drainage department.) Tj T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf (2. Trigger levels) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
T*
(Levels are read from river gauge E-114 at Weir Bridge, which reports every fifteen) Tj T*
(minutes to the control room. Three alert stages are defined.) Tj T*
T*
(Stage 1 \(Watch\) begins at 3.2 metres. The duty manager reviews the forecast every hour) Tj T*
(and confirms that barrier crews are available.) Tj T*
T*
(Stage 2 \(Warning\) begins at 3.8 metres. Barrier crews are mobilised and the incident) Tj T*
(room is opened.) Tj T*
T*
(Stage 3 \(Emergency\) begins at 4.4 metres, or earlier when the forecast shows the river) Tj T*
(will pass that height within six hours. The operations director takes command.) Tj T*
T*
/F2 13 Tf (3. Roles) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
T*
(The duty manager leads at Stages 1 and 2. The operations director, or a deputy named on) Tj T*
(the weekly roster, leads at Stage 3. A communications officer joins the incident room) Tj T*
(from Stage 2 onwards.) Tj T*
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
